== History ==

The tower was completed in 1983 during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] as the headquarters of [[Banc de l'Estret]], a regional bank that had expanded aggressively from its base in the Estret corridor into commercial lending and property finance. The building's pale, institutional exterior and formal composition were characteristic of the prestige office architecture of the early 1980s, and its location in the Districte Financer reflected the bank's ambitions to compete with the larger institutions concentrated along the waterfront.

[[Banc de l'Estret]] was one of the two smaller banks that failed by mid-1988 during the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]], when the collapse of property values exposed the fragility of its loan portfolio. The bank's assets, including the tower, were placed under the control of the restructuring authorities. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]], established in 1994 as Balboa's sovereign wealth fund, acquired the building at a significant discount shortly after its founding. The Fons established its headquarters in the tower and converted the first eight floors to residential units, while retaining the upper floors as office space for its own operations and associated tenants.

== Use ==

The tower operates as a mixed-use building. The first eight floors contain residential units, while floors nine through forty-one serve as the headquarters of the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] and associated office tenants. The lower podium contains the main lobby, retail space, and parking access, while the tiered crown houses mechanical equipment, communications facilities, and executive amenity space.

